  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon-dorsalis-2/",
      "html": "<p>Hyphessobrycon dorsalis circulated in the hobby as \"Hyphessobrycon minor\" before its scientific description in 2014. It is the slimmest representative of the so-called \"Rosy Tetras\", which are characterised within the large group of small tetras by their white-black-white dorsal fin coloration.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon-dorsalis-2/",
      "html": "<p>The fish from which the scientific description is based was imported from Brazil via Manaus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/hyphessobrycon-dorsalis-2/",
      "html": "<p>The species is best kept as a troop of perhaps 15 or 20 specimens in a well planted community aquarium together with colourful classics such as the cardinal tetra. Its discreet, white-wine colours provide contrast and attract at least as much attention as brightly coloured relatives. In the dealer's tank there is usually nothing to be seen of these colours.</p>"
    }
  ],
